   "scholar": "UqKi_qAAAAAJ",
   "bio": "Michael Batty is Emeritus Bartlett Professor of Planning at University College London and Chair of the Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis (CASA). He is a graduate of the University of Manchester in Town and Country Planning and of the University of Wales Institute of Science and Technology in Architecture, where he was Professor of Town Planning and Dean in the 1980s. He was Director of the National Center for Geographic Information and Analysis (NCGIA) at SUNY-Buffalo from 1990 to 1995 before he set up CASA at UCL. He is a Fellow of the British Academy, the Royal Society, the Academy of Social Sciences, and the Royal Town Planning Institute, and he was elected a foreign Academician of the Chinese Academy of Sciences in 2023 and the Indian National Science Academy in 2025. He has worked on computer models of cities and their visualisation since the 1970s. His recent books are Cities and Complexity (2005), The New Science of Cities (2013), Inventing Future Cities (2018), and The Computable City (2024), all from The MIT Press. His edited book Urban Informatics (Springer, 2021) reflects his focus on digital and geospatial technologies in urban planning, and he co-authored A Digital Future for Planning, the report of the Digital Task Force for Planning (2022).",
   "research": "",
   "records_confirmed": false
